1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,818
Your graphics card already plugs into a slot, so why does it need extra power cables?

2
00:00:06,568 --> 00:00:10,487
The PCIe slot can supply up to 75 watts.

3
00:00:10,767 --> 00:00:13,037
That's enough for a small, basic card.

4
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:18,893
But a mid-range or high-end card can pull 300 watts or more while gaming.

5
00:00:19,173 --> 00:00:21,523
The rest comes straight from the power supply.

6
00:00:22,273 --> 00:00:23,841
That's what the cables carry.

7
00:00:24,122 --> 00:00:27,606
A 6-pin connector is rated for 75 watts.

8
00:00:27,886 --> 00:00:29,886
An 8-pin, 150.

9
00:00:30,166 --> 00:00:34,089
The newer 12V-2x6 connector, up to 600.

10
00:00:34,839 --> 00:00:35,652
Add it up.

11
00:00:35,932 --> 00:00:44,042
75 from the slot plus two 8-pins gives 375 watts, enough for a 300 watt card.

12
00:00:44,792 --> 00:00:48,322
So plug in every power connector on the card, firmly.

13
00:00:48,602 --> 00:00:51,756
An empty socket can mean crashes or a black screen.

14
00:00:52,036 --> 00:00:54,602
More PC hardware explained on the channel.

